WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
UDP is message-oriented.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:08.680
A datagram is sent as a unit, and the receiver handles a datagram rather than an arbitrary slice of a byte stream.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.820
This is different from TCP, where the application must frame its own messages over a stream.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.800
UDP preserves a message boundary, but it does not provide many other guarantees.

5
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.760
Delivery is not guaranteed.

6
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:27.740
A datagram can be lost, duplicated, delayed, or arrive in a different order than the sender intended.

7
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.160
UDP also does not provide retransmission, congestion control, or connection state for the application.

8
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:41.460
If any of those properties matter, the application protocol must define them deliberately.

9
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:49.580
C code that uses UDP should handle missing messages, repeated messages, unexpected order, and ordinary network errors.

10
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:58.560
A service should not assume that one request always leads to one response or that a sequence of datagrams will be complete.

11
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:03.380
If the application tracks state, the state machine has to tolerate network reality.

12
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.020
Datagram size and receive-buffer behavior matter.

13
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.800
If the application provides a receive buffer that is smaller than the datagram, platform and API behavior determines what the program observes.

14
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.060
The code should know whether truncation can be detected and how to handle it.

15
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.320
A truncated datagram should not be processed as if it were complete.

16
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.320
Application protocols built on UDP need explicit rules.

17
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.020
Which datagrams are valid?

18
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.880
How are versions handled?

19
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.780
Which messages are idempotent?

20
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.040
How are repeated messages treated?

21
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.660
When is state created, updated, or expired?

22
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:47.720
The protocol may remain small, but its reliability, validation, and state assumptions should be visible enough for review.
